speechtext:
The Indian Department has certain officials in charge of the business of administering Indian affairs in British Columbia, and one of their principal duties is to protect the interests of the Indians. As a matter of fact, the interests of the Indians have not been protected in this case, to say nothing about their removal from the reservation or the money transactions that were gone into. Their buildings on the reserve, which were their private property, were destroyed, I understand, not secretly by any means, but destroyed by order and authority of the British Columbia Government, as exercising proprietory rights on this property. Now, that cannot have occurred without the cognizance of the officials of the department. If it occurred without the cognizance of the officials then these officers should have ceased their connection with the department long ago. If it occurred either with or without their cognizance, they should have ceased their connection long ago, unless this Government, which employs these officers, is prepared to accept its responsibility for the action that the British Columbia Government was allowed to take.
]Mr. Oliver. [
.1 will ask if these men are still in the Government's employ.

I have had a number of returns brought down showing the expenses of Duchemin. I have tried to wade through them but have not been so successful as the hon. member 330}
for North Cape Breton (Mr. McKenzie). The accounts seem to overlap, and it is almost impossible to tell just how they stand, but I happen to have some personal knowledge of some of the investigations Duchemin conducted. In a return brought down on March 5, 1914, Reference No. 273, I find .a detailed statement setting forth that Duchemin investigated ten persons in Antigonish county-sectiotnimen on the Intercolonial. Perhaps hon. gentlemen will be surprised that there are ten sectionmen in the county of Antigonish. This strictly non-partisan railway management only dismissed two out of every three of the section men in my county. These ten investigations took place on the 3rd, 4th, 7th, ftth and 14th of May-five days in all.
There were five days on which these investigations were conducted. He has charged $210.47 and his expenses bring the total up to $261.25. Allowing him for five days' service-and the return shows that that is all the time that he put on these investigations, the dates being given for each of the different officers investigated-he should receive only about $105. I notice that in some of the returns he has charged for investigating Alexander McDonald, $22.50. On the same day that he investigated Alexander McDonald he investigated four others at Arisaig, and I suppose he has put down the same amount, $22.50, against each one of these. There is certainly enough to arouse suspicion of the gravest kind, and I am thoroughly convinced that this innocent Mr. Duchemin padded his account. If an investigation were held I have not the slightest doubt but that it would be shown that the charges that were made by my hon. friend from North Cape Breton (Mr. McKenzie) are correct. I think it would be well for the Government to have a thorough investigation of this matter to get some of their officials after the House prorogues, to go into these accounts thoroughly and see whether there has been overlappng and padding. I have not the slightest doubt that he has been paid three, times over for the service that he has rendered to the Government.
